## Friday evening, FTP

Remember how a site was deployed in 2010: open a program, connect to the server, drag files from one folder to another. What can go wrong here? Everything. One file forgotten. The wrong version uploaded. A broken link unnoticed. A change goes to the live site unchecked, and the earlier version is nowhere. And all of it is known by one person who “knows how to upload”.

The modern answer is called **CI/CD**, and behind the two letters hides a simple idea: let a machine do the deploying, by written rules, the same way every time.

- **CI** (continuous integration): every change is built and checked automatically at once. Small edits, frequent checks, and a mistake is found while it is fresh.
- **CD** (continuous delivery or deployment): a checked version reaches people automatically. With **delivery**, a version is always ready, and a person presses the last “release” button. With **deployment**, the site is updated by itself after green checks. For a small site the second is usually the choice.

For you this means that editing a site turns from a ritual into a **commit**: you saved a change in the repository (you know Git as a way to keep versions), pushed it to the main branch, and everything after that goes by itself.

## The stations of a pipeline

A pipeline is a chain of stations, and each checks or does something. If a station jams (turns red), nothing goes further.

1. **The start**: an event, such as a commit to the `main` branch or an opened pull request.
2. **Checks**: broken links, markup errors, tests, accessibility, the presence of the pages that must exist.
3. **The build**: turns the sources into a finished site (you read about building in the part on JavaScript).
4. **A preview**: a draft at a temporary address so you can look at it with your own eyes. Many platforms make one for every pull request.
5. **The deploy**: replacing the live site, but only when everything before it is green.
6. **A notice**: a message in the chat or by email: “published”, or “failed on the link check”.

Run a change through the diagram. Then jam different stations and compare with deploying by hand: there nobody stops a bad change. Switch on “a mistake the checks can’t see” and press “Roll back”.

<PipelineSim />

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why does the deploy come after the checks and not beside them?</summary>

So that a bad version doesn’t reach the live site: if any check is red, the deploy never starts, and the site stays as it was.

</details>

## What it looks like in a file

A pipeline is written in a file. In GitHub Actions it is a text YAML file in the `.github/workflows/` folder. Here is a file for a site on GitHub Pages (the lesson on publishing shows it at work):

```yaml
name: Check and deploy the site
on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write
jobs:
  check:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v7
      - name: Links and markup
        run: npm run check:links && npm run check:html
      - name: Build the pages
        run: npm run build
      - name: The privacy policy and consent page is there
        run: test -f dist/privacy/index.html
      - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v5
        with:
          path: dist
  deploy:
    needs: check
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment: github-pages
    steps:
      - uses: actions/deploy-pages@v5
```

Read it as instructions. `on: push: branches: [main]`: run on every commit to `main`. There are two **jobs**: `check` (check and build) and `deploy`. The line `needs: check` says the deploy waits for the checks to succeed, and if they are red it won’t run. Each **step** is either a ready-made action (`uses:`, for example getting the code) or a command (`run:`). The version numbers of the actions (`@v7`, `@v5`) keep changing: the current ones are in the documentation (in the sources), and these were the latest releases in October 2026.

Lines such as `npm run check:links` are your own commands: they are defined in the project. What each does depends on the tools you chose: a link checker, a markup checker, a bundler. This textbook is checked the same way: on every change it runs type checks, tests, an accessibility check, a link check and a content check, and only then is the main branch deployed to Pages. If the site has a form, add a check that the privacy policy and consent page exists (a step in the example): that way the important page won’t be lost in the next edit.

GitLab writes the same thing in a file called `.gitlab-ci.yml`, and platforms such as Netlify, Vercel and Cloudflare Pages manage with no file at all: connect a repository, and they build and deploy on every commit themselves. That is the simplest pipeline, and for many sites it is enough.

## Secrets

A pipeline needs rights: to deploy to hosting, to write in the chat. Those rights are kept in **tokens and keys**. The main rule is the same as in Docker: **secrets don’t live in the repository**. Not in the pipeline file, not in the code, not in the README. Colleagues, contractors and sometimes the whole world read a repository, and Git’s history keeps everything for good: a deleted file stays in the old commits.

Secrets go into the **service’s settings** (at GitHub, the “Secrets and variables” section in the repository’s settings). The file holds only a name, such as `secrets.NOTIFY_URL`, and the platform fills in the value at run time, hiding it in the log. Three more habits: give a token the least rights it needs (only deploying), pin the versions of other people’s actions (other people’s code runs with your rights), and remember that pull requests from other people’s copies of the repository don’t get secrets.

<details>
<summary>Check yourself: why can’t you just delete a token from the repository after it got there?</summary>

It stays in the commit history, and anyone who read or copied the history could have seen it. A token that got into a repository counts as leaked: you revoke it and issue a new one.

</details>

## Rolling back

Any pipeline will one day release a bad version: checks don’t see everything. So decide in advance how you will go back.

- **Through Git.** The `git revert` command creates a new commit that undoes the bad one. You push it to `main`, and the same pipeline deploys the earlier behavior. You don’t rewrite the main branch’s history with a forced push: that is how other people’s work gets lost.
- **Through the hosting panel.** Static platforms keep earlier deploys, and you can make one of them live with a button.
- **First bring it back, then investigate.** Minutes of downtime are worth more than a fine understanding of the cause.

The one thing a rollback can’t fix is data: if a bad version spoiled the database, the data has to be restored, and that needs backups (the last lesson of the part).

## Practice

In front of you is your project’s site, and under it a pipeline file with gaps. Finish it: a start on a push to `main`, a `check` job with the build and the checks (links, markup and, if the project has a form, the privacy page), a `deploy` job with `needs: check`, and a secret as `secrets.NAME`. Then write what the build checks for each section of the brief, and how you would roll back.

The file here is only read and checked: Actions can’t be run in the sandbox. In the next lesson you’ll deploy a real site, and this file will be useful to you.

## What was wrong before

In 2010 people were afraid of the word “release”: once a month, at night, with a six-page checklist and one person who had “everything set up”. After a release, a week went on fixing what was found. The idea of continuous integration appeared earlier (in the late 1990s), but it became ordinary for small sites only when free pipelines came with repositories. Now a release is boring, and that is exactly what was wanted.
